Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Did You Reset the Shift Clock?
- Story & Context: Martim Cordeiro was 180 meters beneath the surface repairing trunk line TAT-14 when an unshielded repeater pod leaked screaming FM bell harmonics into his diver comms, mingling with the heavy thud of the support ship's diesel pump. Stunned by the sunlit digital melody ringing through the absolute dark of the ocean floor, he deliberately delayed his decompression ascent by eight minutes to tape the frequency on a handheld micro-cassette recorder while his supervisor furiously hammered on the radio console. The incident nearly cost him his marine certification, but within forty-eight hours the recording was loaded into a DX7 at a Porto Pim beach shack, giving birth to the band's first manic anthem. Hook: "Kill the valve and wind the clock back!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cinematic close-up of a vintage commercial diving helmet resting on hot yellow sand under blinding noon sunshine, water droplets evaporating off the scratched brass visor, with frantic neon-pink Portuguese lyrics and clock numbers spray-painted across the marine-grade metal.

Track 2: The Wall Calendar Bleeds Salt
- Story & Context: Quarantined in the Horta decompression clinic with minor nitrogen narcosis, Soraya Mendes set up her DX7 on a squeaky metal gurney and spent seventy-two frantic hours translating Martim's leaked cable tones into bright, rhythmic marimba arpeggios. As ocean fog crept through the ward windows and encrusted the clinic's outdated wall calendars with damp, stinging salt, she layered jagged dance-punk cowbells over a distorted bassline to drown out the continuous beep of the heart monitors. The resulting track was an exhausted confession of missed summer shifts and cabin fever that the band first tested by blasting it through the clinic's dockside PA system until the harbor master cut the breaker. Hook: "I tore November off the wall just to buy your breath!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Artistic album still of a retro paper calendar hanging against a turquoise-painted concrete dockyard wall, soaked in ocean saltwater, with bold red appointment dates running together like wet dye beneath a glaring tropical midday sun and fluorescent glass chimes floating in mid-air.

Track 3: Quarta-Feira Quebrada
- Story & Context: By August of that year, the Wednesday evening beach gathering at Praia do Almoxarife had ballooned into an unsanctioned festival where over six hundred dancers stomped across crushed sea urchin shells while the band played from the flatbed of a rusted diesel truck. Local dock lore quickly claimed that playing the song’s signature DX7 tubular bell riff directly over the ocean-floor fiber lines at high tide would intercept the transatlantic phone connections of everyone who had missed the morning ferry to Lisbon. Martim led the crowd through twenty minutes of unbroken 140-BPM dance-punk breakdowns, his megaphone feeding back against the incoming breakers as the entire beach danced in synchronized, waterlogged fury. Hook: "The calendar is underwater and we missed the final ferry!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A wide, cinematic photograph of hundreds of sunburned dancers thrashing in turquoise shallow surf beneath towering volcanic sea stacks, kick-drum spray caught in mid-air, a yellow undersea data cable snaking through the waves like a fluorescent serpent toward a blazing white sky.

